 "critic": "Nearly every card read as assembled from a small set of templates with swapped names and locations. Repeated patterns: (1) a bare Friday-fee message ('was at the stall again for the Friday fee the Claws collect, and I am short. Can you do something about it?') with no stakes or specifics; (2) short fixed-price jobs ('500 eddies', '250 eddies on delivery, no questions', 'Convince them. 1000 eddies') with generic placeholders like 'a bar off the market' or 'the ripperdoc'; (3) longer cards built from the same three-option menu ('X, Y, or Z') plus an appended flattery or trust line ('People trust you...', 'You do not know me, and that may be why...') that often does not fit the request; (4) exact duplicate texts across cards, and repeated 'nobody on the row had seen the thief before, so somebody must have given the nod' and 'I had never seen them before'. Longer cards were more legible, more caused and more pulling, but still read as parts glued together. The Claws-collector card from Ivo Popov was the most situated and specific.",
 "sheet": 2
